  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nI propose a fundamentally different approach based on sparse representations and variance maximization. Instead of using step functions with arbitrary patterns, I will represent the function h as a concatenation of function segments that are strategically placed to maximize entropy while minimizing overlap periods. The core insight is that optimal solutions often exhibit self-similarity and specific symmetry properties that can be leveraged.\n\nThe method will:\n- Use a genetic algorithm with guided mutation\n- Employ sparse coordinate representation\n- Maximize the variance at points where h changes\n- Maintain self-similarity between segments\n- Preserve smooth variations between 0 and 1\n\nThe primary motivation is that managing when and where the function changes between 0 and 1 could create aperiodic patterns that reduce maximum overlap.\n\nLimitations:\n- Theoretical guarantee requires careful symmetry handling\n- The segment-based approach might miss optimal solutions\n- It's more challenging to balance self-similarity with anti-correlation\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nThe optimization problem is:\nMinimize max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nSubject to:\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nWith discretization:\nn_points=280 points equally spaced on [0,2]\ndx=2.0/280\nLet S be the set of indices where h(x)=1 (shoulder regions)\n\nDefine objective:\nC_5(h) = (1/dx) * max\u2096 \u2211\u1d62 h[i] * (1-h[i+k mod n_points]) * dx   (adjusted for array boundaries)\n\nKey representations:\nh(x) = g(x) where g(x)=0,1 at discrete points and continuous elsewhere\nBounds: 0 \u2264 g(x) \u2264 1, \u222b\u2080\u00b2 g(x)dx=1\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\nn = 280\ndx = 2.0/n\n\nPhase 1: Validation and Initialization\n- Read initial_h_values if available\n- Project to feasible space ensuring sum(h)*dx=1\n- Compute initial C_5 value\n\nPhase 2: Sparse Representation\n- Represent h using a set of turning points T\n- Connect consecutive points with straight lines\n- Maintain minimum separation between turning points\n\nPhase 3: Hierarchical Optimization\ndef sparse_representation():\n    @key_stages:\n    - Control the density of turning points\n    - Balance curve complexity with evaluation cost\n\ndef evolutionary_search():\n    @key_stages:\n    - Population-based search with adaptive mutation\n    - Focus on regions where covariance was previously high\n    - Use self-similarity as a selection criterion\n\nPhase 4: Refinement\ndef smooth_segmentation():\n    @key_stages:\n    - Apply local optimization at turning points\n    - Adjust to maintain sum constraint\n    - Compare with pruned version\n\nPhase 5: Final Validation\nValidate all constraints:\n0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\nsum(h)*dx \u2248 1\nC_5 evaluated < new threshold\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n\nGENETIC ALGORITHMS:\n- Population size: 15 individuals\n- Generations: determined by time budget\n- Crossover type: adaptive - specialized for segments\n- Mutation type: \n  * Global: gaussian mutation with self-similarity\n  * Local: focused adjustments at high-discrepancy points\n  * Knowledge guided: prioritize known good regions\n\nSPARSIFY FUNCTION:\ndef sparsify(points, target_sum=sum_goal):\n    @key_ops:\n    * Select turning points > 0.1 or < 0.9\n    * Maintain minimum separation\n    * Balance segment count with objective performance\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\nEstimate:\n- Sparse representation: O(n_points)\n- Function evaluation: O(n_points^2)\n- GA iteration: O(15 * (n_points))\n\nMaximum generations: min(budget_s//20, safe_time_bound)\n\nFallback procedure:\n1. Validate parent solution\n2. Apply projection to ensure constraints\n3. Use original parent if all other attempts fail\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\nSuccess criteria:\n1. All h[i] in [0,1] - checked\n2. Sum(h)*dx \u2248 1 - checked\n3. C_5 < parent_candidate - measured\n\nReturn format: \n(h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nThe algorithm will prioritize:\n- Self-similarity preservation\n- Varied segment types\n- Good Fourier coefficient distribution\n- Strategic placement of transitions\n\nThis approach differs from the preceding ones by:\n1) Using sparse representations instead of dense mutation\n2) Stereotypes and symmetries management\n3) Hierarchical segmentation instead of global optimization\n4) Adaptation to vary segment density based on objective performance\n\nThe first stage focuses on building turning point distributions that create consistent anti-correlation behavior across scales and shifts.\n\nKey implementation details:\n1. Constraint projection uses simplex projection for better results\n2. Objective evaluation uses specialized Toeplitz matrix construction for faster correlation calculation\n3. Mutation operators are richly parameterized with crossover probabilities based on current evolution stage\n4. The algorithm prioritizes sections of the function where correlation is currently high, while optimizing the length/pattern of segments\n\nThe approach is to directly model the optimal function as having specific periodic/noisy properties that minimize overlap, rather than treating it as a problem of numerical optimization in a large space.",
